<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Giengen</h1><p class="st-meta">1391 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Giengen an der Brenz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Swabian imperial city on the Brenz, later known worldwide as the home of the Steiff teddy bear.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giengen-an-der-brenz">Giengen</a> grew on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenz-river">Brenz</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Swabia</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> royal town. After a period in which it was pledged to the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-helfenstein">Helfenstein</a>, the townspeople secured their freedom, and by 1391 Giengen was recognised as a free imperial city answerable only to the emperor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council governed the small town and its few villages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giengen-an-der-brenz">Giengen</a> lived from crafts, linen and the market for the surrounding farms, and it adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a> in the 16th century. Its position near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> exposed it to war, and in 1634, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War, it was almost completely burned.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giengen-an-der-brenz">Giengen</a> was annexed by Württemberg, becoming part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a> in 1806. In 1880 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margarete-steiff">Margarete Steiff</a> founded her toy business there, and Giengen an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenz-river">Brenz</a> remains the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steiff">Steiff</a> company.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-giengen.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Giengen" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Karlo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giengen_an_der_Brenz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1391 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Giengen an der Brenz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Giengen an der Brenz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1596&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-giengen" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Giengen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4917 479 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Giengen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5237 735L5229 694M5237 735L5212 730"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 735h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 735h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1596&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-giengen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
